WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.100
Fossils show where people lived, not who had children together.

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.680
That needs DNA. If you carry Neanderthal DNA, when did it get there?

3
00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:15.883
Start with a clue.

4
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:21.363
A draft Neanderthal genome was compared with genomes of living people.

5
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:27.723
It shared more variants, sites where genomes differ, with sampled Eurasians than

6
00:00:27.723 --> 00:00:29.443
with sampled sub-Saharan Africans.

7
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:35.777
The team read this as suggesting interbreeding after some people left Africa.

8
00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:39.577
But a clue is not proof, and it gives no date.

9
00:00:40.857 --> 00:00:45.997
An old African population, split into partly separate groups, could produce some of

10
00:00:45.997 --> 00:00:46.977
it.

11
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.250
So look along a chromosome instead.

12
00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.130
Picture one teal chromosome in a modern human.

13
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.010
It carries one long orange block, inherited from a Neanderthal ancestor.

14
00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.550
At first the block is long and unbroken.

15
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.820
Each time eggs and sperm are made, paired chromosomes swap pieces.

16
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.480
This is recombination.

17
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.080
A swap inside the orange block can split it, in the copy passed to a child.

18
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.510
Generation after generation, the swaps pile up.

19
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.870
Some descendants keep the orange block whole.

20
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.530
Some keep a shorter piece, and some lose it.

21
00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:33.170
Across many descendants, the orange pieces that survive get shorter on average.

22
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.840
That makes length a clock.

23
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.100
Long orange segments mean the mixing happened recently.

24
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.120
Short flecks mean it happened long ago.

25
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:52.113
Today, after far more generations, the Neanderthal pieces in living non-Africans are

26
00:01:52.113 --> 00:01:55.453
short. The cutting has run a long time.

27
00:01:56.467 --> 00:02:01.587
Now meet Ust'-Ishim, a man who lived in western Siberia long before us.

28
00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:05.487
Will his orange segments be longer or shorter than yours?

29
00:02:12.723 --> 00:02:17.203
Longer. Fewer generations of cutting had passed since the mixing.

30
00:02:18.543 --> 00:02:22.783
From their lengths, Fu and colleagues worked out how long before his life the gene

31
00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:23.783
flow came.

32
00:02:24.483 --> 00:02:29.423
At Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, three early Homo sapiens were sampled.

33
00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:34.583
Each had a Neanderthal ancestor only a few generations back.

34
00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:39.283
That was a separate, recent contact in their own family trees.

35
00:02:40.643 --> 00:02:42.803
Their orange runs are the longest of all.

36
00:02:43.783 --> 00:02:45.163
Line the three up.

37
00:02:46.463 --> 00:02:47.843
Bacho Kiro, long runs.

38
00:02:49.243 --> 00:02:50.763
Ust'-Ishim, medium pieces.

39
00:02:52.083 --> 00:02:53.803
A living person, short flecks.

40
00:02:55.083 --> 00:02:56.443
The surplus was a clue.

41
00:02:57.663 --> 00:02:59.363
The lengths test it, and say when.

42
00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:02.633
One caution travels with this clock.

43
00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:06.733
It gives an average, for one main period of mixing.

44
00:03:07.913 --> 00:03:10.893
And it says when, not how much ancestry came across.
